  "period_note": "Solo basis. Own contemporaneous annual report: 'Five Year's Performance of RBL' table (own/current-year column) plus Note 7.10-7.11. Cross-checked against the FY2022 report's FY2021 comparative column: balance sheet, capital and bottom-line P&L all agree exactly, EXCEPT Non-interest income (1,709.76 here vs. 1,658.84 in the FY2022 report's comparative) and Non-interest expenses (1,256.77 here vs. 1,205.85 there) - an apparent ~50.92 crore reclassification between the two categories between report vintages, but Net Non-interest income (452.99) and operating profit (118.66) are identical in both, so the reclassification does not affect any validated identity. This report's own contemporaneous split is used per the standing rule. Provision shortfall (loans+OBS): required 47,908,829,747 Taka vs. maintained 17,125,379,053 Taka (maintained derived as required + reported shortfall of -30,783,450,694 Taka = BDT 3,078.35 crore; basis: calculated), a roughly 3x jump from FY2020's shortfall in a single year. The bank's own capital-adequacy note additionally discloses a combined Bangladesh Bank forbearance of BDT 3,090.48 crore against required provision plus BDT 54.10 crore (Deferred Tax Assets) and BDT 10.32 crore (Intangible Assets) - i.e. total regulatory relief materially exceeding this single loans+OBS figure once other asset categories are included. Note 7.11 also discloses a single-obligor special required provision of BDT 842.17 crore against 'Loan in favour of Bangladesh Sugar and Food Industry' (a state-owned sugar-mills borrower) - a major concentration exposure. Reported NPL% (18.84%) vs. computed (17.51%), 1.33pp gap.",
